WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.507
TCP numbers every byte it sends, so the receiver can put bytes in order and spot gaps.

2
00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:12.356
But first, both sides must agree where the numbers start.

3
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:19.293
The payment service picks a random starting number, say 1,000, and sends a SYN, short for synchronize.

4
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.047
The ledger picks its own, 5,000, and replies with a SYN-ACK.

5
00:00:24.047 --> 00:00:27.684
It acknowledges 1,001, the next byte it expects.

6
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.011
The client acknowledges 5,001.

7
00:00:30.011 --> 00:00:32.966
Now both sides know both starting numbers.

8
00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:36.624
That took one round trip, and no data has moved yet.

9
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:41.424
Now the client sends a 100-byte payment authorization.

10
00:00:41.424 --> 00:00:44.655
Its bytes are numbered 1,001 to 1,100.

11
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:51.023
The ledger's kernel acknowledges 1,101: everything up to 1,100 has arrived.

12
00:00:51.023 --> 00:00:55.291
Without that, the client would send the bytes again.

13
00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:01.063
To close, each side sends a FIN, short for finish, and the other side acknowledges it.

14
00:01:01.063 --> 00:01:02.745
The client finishes first.

15
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:06.173
The ledger acknowledges it, then sends its own FIN.

16
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.311
The client acknowledges that, and the ledger closes the connection.

17
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:12.460
The client doesn't close yet.

18
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.580
It waits in a state called TIME_WAIT, for 60 seconds on Linux.

19
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.041
There are two reasons.

20
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:20.965
First, the client's last message might be lost.

21
00:01:20.965 --> 00:01:26.131
The ledger would then send its FIN again, and the client must still be there to answer it.

22
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:30.559
Second, a late copy of an old packet may still be in the network.

23
00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:34.135
It must not land in a new connection on the same ports.

24
00:01:34.135 --> 00:01:35.825
Waiting lets it die first.

25
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:38.993
Only the side that closes first waits.

26
00:01:38.993 --> 00:01:47.716
A Linux client has about 28,000 ports, so more than 470 new connections a second to one server runs out of them.

27
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:53.636
Keep connections open and reuse them, and you pay for the handshake and the wait only once.
